<article class="env-lemma" id="00EC">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00EC">Lemma <span data-tag="00EC">10.21.1</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a ring. Let $e \in R$ be an idempotent. In this case </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R) = D(e) \amalg D(1-e).  \]
</div>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Note that an idempotent $e$ of a domain is either $1$ or $0$. Hence we see that 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \begin{eqnarray*}  D(e) &  = &  \{  \mathfrak p \in \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R) \mid e \not\in \mathfrak p \}  \\ &  = &  \{  \mathfrak p \in \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R) \mid e \not= 0\text{ in }\kappa (\mathfrak p) \}  \\ &  = &  \{  \mathfrak p \in \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R) \mid e = 1\text{ in }\kappa (\mathfrak p) \}  \end{eqnarray*}
</div>
    <p>
       Similarly we have 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \begin{eqnarray*}  D(1-e) &  = &  \{  \mathfrak p \in \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R) \mid 1 - e \not\in \mathfrak p \}  \\ &  = &  \{  \mathfrak p \in \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R) \mid e \not= 1\text{ in }\kappa (\mathfrak p) \}  \\ &  = &  \{  \mathfrak p \in \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R) \mid e = 0\text{ in }\kappa (\mathfrak p) \}  \end{eqnarray*}
</div>
    <p>
       Since the image of $e$ in any residue field is either $1$ or $0$ we deduce that $D(e)$ and $D(1-e)$ cover all of $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R)$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>